the more we know about biology the more  that we understand that you can't really  study biology in isolation to get a firm  understanding of how biological systems  evolve so EB major is highly  interdisciplinary in terms of the  students can take courses and hear  lectures and experience fieldwork where  we cover things from mathematical  understandings of biological systems  obtaining gene sequences and genomic  data including traditional techniques  and next-generation sequencing they can  learn about synthetic biology ways that  researchers are literally being able to  create life in the laboratory you have  to understand the underlying chemistry  physics there might be engineering  principles there are many different  facets taken from other sciences that  merge very well with biology modern  biology for us to understand the natural  world and how organisms will evolve  within it I would say the EE B major  should very much appeal to students who  are excited about research so students  can take what they have learned in the  classroom and construct research  projects to go out to their favorite  habitats and this could range from  desert habitats to the poles to the  oceans basically anywhere that students  can use their imagination and examine  the organisms that thrive in those  habitats and get hands-on experience  being in the field this is something  that we pride ourselves on and ideally  their research could even be published  in a major journal so right now we're  inside the hidden part of the Yale  Peabody Museum of Natural History and  this is where Yale houses its vertebrate  collections within the museum so this  ranges from fish specimens birds  reptiles mammals all sorts of creatures  that have been gathered from around the  world for a good reason for the purpose  of conducting research and better  understanding the ecology and evolution  living world so our major prepares  students for a variety of things that  they might want to do in the future we  have students that are pre-med pre-vet  pre-dental so a lot of the things that  you might associate with a biology  degree but also we recognize that  students may be attracted and prepared  to go into industry so they could work  in terms of finding new biofuels and  doing work and bioremediation but there  are all sorts of vital skills that we  provide in terms of understanding  biology both of the organisms that  surround us as well as ourselves that  will help prepare students no matter  what it is that they wants to do in the  future
 

